// The Core-Wasm checkpoint contract `hologram:guest/core-wasm@3` (HO-47).
//
// The session world `@2` with the state made addressable. The guest is an
// import-free wasm32 core module, not a component. It exports exactly five
// items: `memory`, `holo_alloc`, `holo_step`, `holo_state` and
// `holo_restore`. WIT cannot name an exported memory, so `memory` is stated
// here and not declared below.
//
// WIT names are kebab-case and name the core exports with underscores. In the
// canonical ABI, u32 lowers to i32 and u64 lowers to i64, so the core
// signatures are exactly:
//
//   holo_alloc:   (i32) -> i32
//   holo_step:    (i32, i32) -> i64
//   holo_state:   () -> i64
//   holo_restore: (i32) -> i32
//
// A step is the step of `@2`. `holo-state` locates the resident state's bytes
// as `offset << 32 | length` and changes nothing, so a host can name the state
// by the BLAKE3 of those bytes. `holo-restore` reserves `length` bytes as the
// state of a fresh instance and returns their offset; the host writes the
// state there before the first step. It traps on an instance that has
// reserved a request, taken a step or restored before, and on a length over
// the declared state maximum. A trap ends the session.
package hologram:guest@3.0.0;

world core-wasm {
  /// Reserve `length` bytes for the next request and return their offset. Discards the previous step's responses.
  export holo-alloc: func(length: u32) -> u32;

  /// Take one session step over the request at `offset` with `length` bytes. Returns `offset << 32 | length` of the responses.
  export holo-step: func(offset: u32, length: u32) -> u64;

  /// Return `offset << 32 | length` of the resident state's bytes. Changes nothing.
  export holo-state: func() -> u64;

  /// Reserve `length` bytes as the state of a fresh instance and return their offset.
  export holo-restore: func(length: u32) -> u32;
}
